WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.385
This lesson becomes much easier when the graph is read as a progress log rather than as a sketch of the path.

2
00:00:05.385 --> 00:00:10.769
The vertical axis tells how much distance has been completed by a certain time.

3
00:00:10.769 --> 00:00:16.154
The horizontal axis tells how much time has passed.

4
00:00:16.154 --> 00:00:21.538
So the line is not showing hills, corners, or bends in the route.

5
00:00:21.538 --> 00:00:26.923
It is showing how progress accumulates as the mission clock runs.

6
00:00:26.923 --> 00:00:32.307
Once that distinction is secure, the graph starts to read like a motion journal instead of a confusing map.

7
00:00:32.307 --> 00:00:37.692
Keep this takeaway in view: A distance-time graph is a record of accumulated progress, not a picture of the route itself.

8
00:00:37.692 --> 00:00:43.076
Vertical axis means distance.

9
00:00:43.076 --> 00:00:48.461
Graph height tells the recorded distance by that time.

10
00:00:48.461 --> 00:00:53.845
Horizontal axis means time.

11
00:00:53.845 --> 00:00:59.230
The mission clock runs from left to right.

12
00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:05.720
Look at the mission-log graph carefully.

13
00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:12.210
A higher point means more distance has been recorded by that time.

14
00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:18.700
A rising straight segment means the traveller is moving steadily because distance is being added at a constant rate.

15
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:25.190
A flat section means the time is still increasing while the distance stays fixed, so the traveller is stopped.

16
00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:31.680
The visual labels matter because they pin the meaning directly to the graph features and stop the mind from drifting back toward map thinking.

17
00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:38.170
Keep this takeaway in view: Graph height and graph shape work together to tell the motion story.

18
00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:44.660
Height: A higher point means a larger recorded distance by that time.

19
00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:51.150
Rising line: Distance is being added as time passes, so motion is happening.

20
00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:56.251
A helpful analogy is a quest journal.

21
00:01:56.251 --> 00:02:01.352
Each chapter records how much ground has been completed by a certain moment.

22
00:02:01.352 --> 00:02:06.453
A rising chapter means progress is being added.

23
00:02:06.453 --> 00:02:11.554
A flat chapter means time is passing with no new progress.

24
00:02:11.554 --> 00:02:16.655
A steeper chapter means progress is being added more quickly.

25
00:02:16.655 --> 00:02:21.755
This analogy matters because it turns the graph into a time-ordered story log, which is exactly how the worked example needs to be read.

26
00:02:21.755 --> 00:02:26.856
Keep this takeaway in view: Read the graph like a journal of progress chapters, one segment at a time.

27
00:02:26.856 --> 00:02:31.957
Rising chapter means moving.

28
00:02:31.957 --> 00:02:37.058
The traveller adds progress while the mission clock advances.

29
00:02:37.058 --> 00:02:42.159
Flat chapter means paused.

30
00:02:42.159 --> 00:02:47.260
The story continues in time, but the traveller is not adding distance.

31
00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:52.686
This lesson also locks in the first important graph-gradient idea.

32
00:02:52.686 --> 00:02:58.112
On a distance-time graph, the slope is change in distance divided by change in time.

33
00:02:58.112 --> 00:03:03.538
That gives the pace, or speed, on that segment.

34
00:03:03.538 --> 00:03:08.963
So if one straight segment is steeper than another, it represents faster motion.

35
00:03:08.963 --> 00:03:14.389
A zero slope means no change in distance, which is why a flat section means a stop.

36
00:03:14.389 --> 00:03:19.815
The same geometry can mean something else on a different graph type later, but here the axes make slope belong to pace.

37
00:03:19.815 --> 00:03:25.241
Keep this takeaway in view: On a distance-time graph, steepness is pace, not position and not route shape.

38
00:03:25.241 --> 00:03:30.667
More steep: More distance is added each second, so the pace is larger.

39
00:03:30.667 --> 00:03:36.092
Less steep: Distance still increases, but more slowly.

40
00:03:36.092 --> 00:03:41.518
pace = slope = change in distance / change in time.

41
00:03:41.518 --> 00:03:46.944
The axes decide the meaning.

42
00:03:46.944 --> 00:03:52.370
Here, slope belongs to speed or pace.

43
00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:57.590
The comparison graph deepens the lesson.

44
00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:02.810
Two runs can finish at the same final distance and at the same final time, but still have different pace histories.

45
00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:08.030
One traveller may pause and then catch up.

46
00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:13.250
Another may keep one steady slope throughout.

47
00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:18.470
The final point alone cannot reveal the whole motion story.

48
00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:23.690
That is why graph reading must be done section by section instead of by glancing only at the ending.

49
00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:28.910
Keep this takeaway in view: The final point is not the whole story; the segments between the endpoints matter too.

50
00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:34.130
Run A: Contains a flat paused section and then catches up later.

51
00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:39.350
Run B: Keeps one steady slope from start to finish.

52
00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:44.862
A common trap is to look at a high point on the graph and say the traveller must be moving fast there.

53
00:04:44.862 --> 00:04:50.374
That is not what graph height means on a distance-time graph.

54
00:04:50.374 --> 00:04:55.887
Height tells how much distance has already been covered.

55
00:04:55.887 --> 00:05:01.399
Pace comes from the slope.

56
00:05:01.399 --> 00:05:06.911
A line can sit high on the graph and still be flat, which would mean the traveller is stopped at that time.

57
00:05:06.911 --> 00:05:12.423
Keeping height and slope in separate mental boxes is one of the biggest wins of this lesson.

58
00:05:12.423 --> 00:05:17.936
Keep this takeaway in view: Distance-time graphs answer the distance question with height and the pace question with slope.

59
00:05:17.936 --> 00:05:23.448
Height tells recorded distance.

60
00:05:23.448 --> 00:05:28.960
Slope tells pace on that segment.

61
00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:32.361
Try three checks.

62
00:05:32.361 --> 00:05:35.761
What does a flat section mean?

63
00:05:35.761 --> 00:05:39.162
A stop, because time passes while distance stays the same.

64
00:05:39.162 --> 00:05:42.563
What does a steeper rising section mean?

65
00:05:42.563 --> 00:05:45.964
Faster motion, because more distance is added each second.

66
00:05:45.964 --> 00:05:49.364
And if two graphs finish at the same final point, must the journeys have been identical?

67
00:05:49.364 --> 00:05:52.765
No.

68
00:05:52.765 --> 00:05:56.166
Their pace histories can still differ.

69
00:05:56.166 --> 00:05:59.566
Those three checks are enough to make the example feel much more straightforward.

70
00:05:59.566 --> 00:06:02.967
Keep this takeaway in view: Segment meaning first, whole story second.

71
00:06:02.967 --> 00:06:06.368
Flat section means stopped.

72
00:06:06.368 --> 00:06:09.769
Time increases while distance stays unchanged.

73
00:06:09.769 --> 00:06:13.169
Steeper rise means faster.

74
00:06:13.169 --> 00:06:16.570
The pace is greater on that segment.

75
00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:21.453
You are ready for the worked example now.

76
00:06:21.453 --> 00:06:26.336
A graph rises from zero to twenty-four metres in six seconds, stays flat for four seconds, then rises from twenty-four metres to thirty-six metres in three seconds.

77
00:06:26.336 --> 00:06:31.219
Read it one segment at a time.

78
00:06:31.219 --> 00:06:36.101
First rising segment: steady motion.

79
00:06:36.101 --> 00:06:40.984
Flat segment: stop.

80
00:06:40.984 --> 00:06:45.867
Final rising segment: moving again.

81
00:06:45.867 --> 00:06:50.750
Because the final rise is steeper than the first one, the traveller moves again and this time faster than before.

82
00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:55.633
So the full story is move steadily, stop, then move again faster than before.

83
00:06:55.633 --> 00:07:00.516
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: read the mission log like a timed story of progress.

84
00:07:00.516 --> 00:07:05.399
Step 1: Read each segment in order from left to right.

85
00:07:05.399 --> 00:07:10.281
Step 2: Use flat and steepness clues to identify stop and faster motion.

86
00:07:10.281 --> 00:07:15.164
rise -> move steadily.

87
00:07:15.164 --> 00:07:20.047
flat -> stopped.

88
00:07:20.047 --> 00:07:24.930
Tell the story segment by segment instead of staring only at the final point.
